Fully thirty thousand people gathered
in the vicinity of Union station in Richmond yesterday and cheered the Virgin
ia troops as the trains pulled out for the
front, leaving Camp Lee deserted.
Every housetop in the neighborhood,
every bridge or other place of vantage
from which could be gotten a glimpse
of the scene was densely crowded. The
churches were almost empty in the
morning, and thousands lined the
streets over which the troops passed on
their way to the depot.


The Third Regiment left in three
sections about six o'clock over the Rich.
mond, Fredericksburg and Potomac
Railroad for Camp Alger, at Falls
Church, while the Fourth left about four
hours later over the Seaboard Air Line
for Jacksonville. The following is a
list of the companies constituting the
Third Regiment.


Third—Col. VIM. Male, commanding;
Company A, Danville, Captain Hutchins;
Company B, Culpeper, Captain Grimsley;
Company C, Farmville, Captain Allen;
Company D, Charlottesville, Captain Wingfield;
Company E, Lynchburg, Captain Faulkner;
Company F, Alexandria, Capt. Atkinson;
Company G, Petersburg, Capt. Nichols;
Company H, Danville, Capt. Freeman;
Company I, Fairfax, Capt. Willard;
Company K, Fredericksburg, Capt. Rowe;
Company L, [missing], Capt. Miller;
Company N, Richmond, Capt. Masuroer.


The Third Regiment crowded the
cars, which were side-tracked about
fifty yards from the camp. Despite the
early hour there were thousands of
persons at Camp Lee to bid them farewell.

